Third Session
Five Policy Questions

Question: The Book of Documents (Shu) states: "Great Heaven has conferred its moral sense on the lower people. Compliance with their constant nature and the ability to tranquilize their way belong solely to the sovereign." This means that human nature is originally good, yet cultivating the Way and establishing instruction so as to cause all people to rest securely in their proper path and return to goodness depends upon the ruler. Examining antiquity, the rulers who guided the people toward goodness and were able to fulfill the responsibility of tranquilizing their way were Yao, Shun, Tang, and Wu. Can the methods by which they guided them be stated? In later ages, there was one who personally inscribed the Classic of Filial Piety (Xiaojing) on the reverse of a stone tablet, and there was one who personally inscribed the Mencius (Mengzi) upon a screen—which rulers were these? And were such acts also capable of guiding the people toward goodness? Reverently we reflect on how Emperor Gao, the Grand Progenitor (Taizu Gao Huangdi), compiled the three parts of the Grand Pronouncements (Dagao) and promulgated them throughout the realm; assuredly this served to inspire and awaken people's innate goodness and succeeded in tranquilizing their way, following the exact same principles as Yao, Shun, Tang, and Wu. Can the essentials by which he guided the people also be stated?…
